---
import Panel from "../components/Panel.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { lastSync } from "../lib/db";
import { ago, SITE_NAME } from "../lib/format";

const synced = await lastSync();
---

<Layout title="About the data" crumbs={[{ label: "About the data" }]}>
  <div class="console">
    <Panel>
      <div class="panel-body head">
        <p class="label"><span data-scramble>How this site works</span></p>
        <h1 class="cursor"><span data-scramble>About the data</span></h1>
        <p class="intro">
          {SITE_NAME} is a free, independent tracker of the U.S. Congress. It isn't affiliated with Congress or any government agency. Data refreshes every hour; the last refresh was {ago(synced)}.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Panel>

    <div class="docs">
      <Panel label="Sources">
        <div class="panel-body prose">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Bills and resolutions:</strong> Congress.gov data via GovInfo's bill status files (public domain): status, actions, sponsors, cosponsors, subjects, committees, and CRS summaries.</li>
            <li><strong>House votes:</strong> Office of the Clerk, U.S. House of Representatives.</li>
            <li><strong>Senate votes and DW-NOMINATE scores:</strong> Lewis, Jeffrey B., Keith Poole, Howard Rosenthal, Adam Boche, Aaron Rudkin, and Luke Sonnet. <em>Voteview: Congressional Roll-Call Votes Database.</em> <a class="link" href="https://voteview.com/" rel="noopener">voteview.com</a>. Senate member-level votes can trail the Senate by up to a month.</li>
            <li><strong>Members, contact details, and portraits:</strong> the @unitedstates project's congress-legislators data and official member photos (public domain).</li>
            <li><strong>Regulations and executive orders:</strong> the Federal Register API.</li>
            <li><strong>District lookup:</strong> the U.S. Census Bureau geocoder.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <Panel label="Method">
        <div class="panel-body prose">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Status</strong> comes from each bill's official action codes: introduced, reported by committee, passed one chamber, passed both, sent to the President, vetoed, or became law. Resolutions are "agreed to" when adopted.</li>
            <li><strong>Votes with party</strong> uses the standard party-unity measure: on votes where most Democrats and most Republicans voted opposite ways, how often a member sided with their party. Independents are counted with Democrats, with whom both current Independents caucus.</li>
            <li><strong>Votes needed to pass</strong> follow each vote's rule: a simple majority; two-thirds for House "suspension" votes (a fast track for bills with broad support) and veto overrides; or 60 senators for cloture (ending debate).</li>
            <li><strong>Vice-presidential tie-breakers</strong> aren't part of member-level vote records; the official result is shown with each tally.</li>
            <li><strong>Summaries</strong> are written by the nonpartisan Congressional Research Service. Some bills also have a plain-English overview written by AI from the bill text; it's always labelled as AI-written and kept apart from the official summary.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </Panel>

      <div class="stack aside">
        <Panel label="Privacy">
          <div class="panel-body prose">
            <p>There are no accounts, ads, or trackers. Addresses entered on <a class="link" href="/find">Find my rep</a> are sent only to the Census Bureau's geocoder to find your district, and aren't stored or logged.</p>
          </div>
        </Panel>

        <Panel label="Credits">
          <div class="panel-body prose">
            <p>Typefaces: Switzer by Indian Type Foundry (ITF Free Font License) and Reddit Mono (SIL Open Font License). Interface inspired by the console aesthetic of data-monitoring tools.</p>
          </div>
        </Panel>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .head h1 {
    margin: 10px 0 14px;
  }
  .intro {
    max-width: 66ch;
    padding-left: 16px;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--line-2);
    color: #bdbdbd;
  }
  /* Sources and Method side by side, with the two short notes stacked in a third column;
     on tablets the short notes run across underneath, and on phones everything stacks. */
  .docs {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--gap);
    align-items: start;
    margin-top: var(--gap);
  }
  @media (max-width: 1100px) {
    .docs {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
    .aside {
      grid-column: 1 / -1;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .docs,
    .aside {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
